{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And he looked, and behold, a ram.</b> This ram was one of the ten things created at twilight. Therefore it is called איל and not כבש as in the language of the sacrifices, כבשים בני שנה — because they subdue (כובשין) the iniquities of Israel. For a sheep that is older in years is called איל, and when it is a day old it is called כבש, as it is said (Leviticus 22): ״שור או כבש״ and so forth. Moreover, this ram was offered as an עולה, and in it there is an allusion to Knesset Israel, which is called אילת השחר — the dawn's hind — for she is the hind of Israel, receiving from the dawn (שחר); she shields them in every generation through the merit of the binding (עקדה), and she receives first." "<b>Behind, caught in a thicket by his horns.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): after the angel said to him, 'Do not send your hand against the lad,' he saw the sheep caught in a thicket by its horns. And the word בסבך teaches that that place was a forest, and there were trees there; and it is the place called בית יער הלבנון — the house of the forest of Lebanon — and this is what David said concerning the Shekhinah (Psalms 132): ״מצאנוה בשדי יער״. And behold, it is in the portion of Benjamin, of whom it is written (Genesis 49): ״בנימין זאב יטרף״." "<b>And</b> by way of the Midrash: אחר נאחז בסבך בקרניו — after Israel is caught in transgressions all the days of the year and entangled in troubles, they take on Rosh HaShanah the shofar of the ram. After Israel is caught in transgressions and entangled in kingdoms — from Babylonia to Media, and from Media to Greece, and from Greece to Edom — their end is to be redeemed through the horn of the ram, as it is said (Zechariah 9): ״ה׳ בשופר יתקע״." "<b>And know</b> the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory, that the shofar of the giving of the Torah, of which it is written וקול שופר חזק מאד — 'and the voice of the shofar grew exceedingly mighty' — was the shofar of the ram of Isaac. And on Rosh HaShanah, which is the day of judgment, we were commanded to sound the shofar, and even though we learned 'all shofars are valid,' the mitzvah in the most preferred manner is with that of a ram." "<b>And know</b> that their saying, 'it was the shofar of the ram of Isaac,' its intent concerns the attribute of Isaac, not his body and bones literally — for this ram was entirely offered as a whole burnt offering, with its horns and hooves. Rather, the Sages allude to us concerning the attribute of גבורה, which we heard from His mouth: אנכי and לא יהיה לך — 'I am' and 'You shall have no other god' — and this is פחד יצחק, 'the Fear of Isaac,' which we seek in time of trouble and say, עננו פחד יצחק — 'Answer us, O Fear of Isaac.' It is the voice heard at the giving of the Torah, and to it we allude with the sound of the shofar that we sound on Rosh HaShanah, which is the day on which the world was created. And reflect: the day of the giving of the Torah is the beginning of the acceptance of His kingship, and likewise the day on which the world was created is the beginning of His kingship, for there is no king without a host." "<b>And you need to know</b> that what you find in this passage — that the One who tests is Elohim, and the angel of HaShem, blessed be He, prevents — the matter is by way of the Kabbalah; the eyes of the heart shall be opened. For this angel is not of the separate beings, but of the 'growings' (נטיעות) which cannot be severed or cut; for were he of the separate beings, Abraham would not have heeded his voice in nullifying what Elohim had commanded him, nor would the angel have been authorized to say ״ולא חשכת ממני״ — 'you have not withheld from me' — but rather 'from Him.' But the angel was of the 'growings'; this is the great angel, and it is of him that it is said (Exodus 14) ״ויסע מלאך האלהים״, and he cleaves only in Light. And the reason it mentioned him here as 'angel' is to say: an angel within whom is HaShem — and it is of him that it is said ״כי שמי בקרבו״ — 'for My name is within him.' And from this it mentioned him in the verse (Genesis 48) ״המלאך הגואל אתי״ — 'the angel who redeemed me' — etc., 'within the land'; and he is HaShem in whom is mercy, and because he is HaShem he spoke in the language of 'from me' (ממני), and because he is mercy he said after the offering ״כי עתה ידעתי כי ירא אלהים אתה ולא חשכת את בנך את יחידך ממני... וברך אברכך והרבה ארבה... כי באשר שמעת בקולי... נאם ה׳״ — 'by Myself have I sworn, says YHWH.' And so you find that before the offering it mentioned ״אלהים יראה לו השה״, and after the offering it mentioned ״השם יראה״, and promised him with the attribute of mercy that He would bless his seed with mighty increase. And I observed the language of blessing and found it doubled." "<b>And it is known</b> that it is an allusion to power within power, in the manner of song and praise — and it is what He said: ״כי ברך אברכך והרבה ארבה״, and He said ״ככוכבי השמים וכחול אשר על שפת הים״, and He says ״ויירש זרעך״, and ״והתברכו בזרעך״. And He mentioned at the end ״עקב אשר שמעת בקולי״ — 'because you listened to My voice' — and My voice is the Elohim who commands." "<b>And he offered him up for a burnt-offering instead of his son.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: what is <b>instead of his son</b>? Rather, for every act of service that he performed with it, he would say: May it be Your will that this act of service be as though I performed it upon my son — as though my son were slaughtered, as though his blood were dashed, as though my son were flayed, as though he were burnt and reduced to ashes."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :register {:fixes 2}, :notes {0 "The phrase אילותן של ישראל המקבלת מן השחר is textually difficult: the antecedent of 'she receives' is unclear; translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:33.538225197Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}